nother X-square! Bad move. It borders A8, giving White easy access.\n\n*   **(C, 7):** This flips several pieces, but it\'s near the edge. I don\'t like giving White easier access to the edges.\n\n*   **(G, 7):** X-square by the H8 corner! It\'s better to avoid this to prevent easy corner gain for the opponent.\n\n*   **(H, 7):** A C-square! It is just beside the H8 corner, which is dangerous, and it is a bad choice.\n\n**After carefully weighing these options, I\'m confident that (B, 5) is the best choice.** It\'s a "perfect hole" move: completely surrounded. This move maintains my position, and does not open any potential plays for White.\n\nNow, I will confirm my chosen answer based on the given coordinate system.\n\nEvaluation: Move (B, 5) is an excellent positional move.\nReason: It is a "perfect hole" move, meaning the square is completely surrounded by existing pieces. Playing here does not increase the frontier (empty squares adjacent to my pieces) and flips only one opponent piece, restricting the opponent\'s mobility while avoiding dangerous C-squares and X-squares like A7, B2, or G7.\n\n**My Final Answer: (B, 5)**\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The move (B, 5) is the strongest option available.\nThe available moves include several structurally weak positions: (A, 7), (H, 7) are C-squares, and (B, 2), (B, 7), (G, 7) are X-squares; playing in these squares adjacent to corners often allows the opponent to capture the corner in subsequent turns.\nThis leaves (B, 3), (B, 5), and (C, 7) as the primary candidates.\n(B, 3) is a decent internal move but borders empty squares (A2, B2), slightly expanding the frontier.\n(C, 7) flips multiple pieces (C5, C6, D6) and is close to the edge, potentially increasing the opponent\'s options.\n(B, 5) is a "hole-filler" move. The square (B, 5) is completely surrounded by occupied pieces. Playing here does not expose any new empty squares to the opponent and flips only one piece (C5). This minimizes the opponent\'s mobility (a